# Chaos: visual effects hooks (src/client/creature.h/.cpp, mapview.h/.cpp, uimap.h, luafunctions_client.cpp, and Lua:
# mods/client_settings/classes/dataset.lua, options/graphics.otui; after 0001-0125). Owner overlay module game_visualfx
# (client/MANIFEST.md "Visual effects") does the rest. Four options in Options > Graphics, all on by default:
#  * Hit flash: Creature:flashHit(shader, ms) draws the outfit with an outfit shader (chaos_hit_white / chaos_hit_red) until
#    the time is up, then the creature's own shader again. Lua tells which creature took a damage number (g_map.onImpactText of 0100).
#  * Screen shake: UIMap.setShake(x, y), a pixel shift (1x sprite pixels, scaled to the sprite size) added to the framebuffer
#    source rect in MapView::calcFramebufferSource, so the picture, the light, the names and the clicks all move together.
#    The draw dimension keeps a margin of at least a tile around the visible area, so a shift of a few pixels shows no edge.
#    The module caps it at 4 px and 180 ms and runs a 16 ms timer only while it shakes.
#  * Soft shadow: Creature.setSoftShadow(bool); Creature::draw queues one 64x32 procedural ellipse (alpha 0 at the rim, built once
#    on the first draw, uploaded by Texture like any other) at the creature's feet before its outfit; as wide as the look's
#    tiles. One extra textured rect per visible creature, nothing when off.
#  * Vignette and colour grade: no C++; a map shader (data/shaders/chaos_map_*.frag) set on the map panel by the module.
# The option values live in dataset.lua (hitFlash, screenShake, creatureShadow, screenGrade); turning "Enable shaders" off
# clears the base's map shader, so its apply asks the module to set the grade again.
# Not run in a full build or in play; the touched files pass g++ -fsyntax-only with the patch chain applied (the only errors
# are the base's own g_app declarations of a build without the framework defines).
